From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 05:20:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(payments): apply the waterfall across every open
 installment, not just the first
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

payment-posting.ts fetched exactly one open schedule row (ORDER BY due_on,
id LIMIT 1) and ran the entire payment against it. A payment covering more
than one overdue installment only cleared the first: the remainder went to
overpayment_holdings, which nothing ever reads back. The result: a client
who paid everything owed still showed a still-open, still-overdue second
installment, which the overdue-detection cron kept flagging.

Reproduced live: two overdue 50,000 installments, one 100,000 payment.
Before: installment 1 paid, installment 2 untouched, 50,000 stranded in
overpayment_holdings. After: both installments paid, nothing held.

Fix: lock every open installment for the loan (still one query, still one
FOR UPDATE, same lock order every time so no new deadlock risk — verified
under real concurrency) and walk the waterfall across them in due-date
order, carrying the remainder from one installment into the next. Only
money left over after every open installment is satisfied becomes
overpaymentAmount. payments.schedule_id (nullable, no behavior elsewhere
depends on it being exclusive) is set to the first installment touched,
same as before for the common single-installment case.

Adds two regression tests (full multi-installment catch-up, and a partial
catch-up that leaves the second installment correctly partial) plus a
concurrency check with two real concurrent catch-up payments.
